decisions.detail:
• The RTO must send quarterly AVETMISS data reports to ASQA

• The RTO must retain records of completed student assessments for a period 12 months commencing on the date which a student is issued with a qualification

• The RTO must retain a quarterly register of workplaces used to deliver training and assessment in relevant courses on scope, and must provide this register on request, including the following details:

• name of workplace;

• address and contact details of workplace;

• course code(s) and title(s) delivered at the workplace;

• trainer(s) and assessor(s) assigned to deliver at the workplace;

• details of students undertaking training at the workplace including a) student name b) date of birth c) student USI d) period of training at the workplace; and

• a sample of photo and video evidence of the facilities while in use.

• The RTO must maintain a register of student complaints. The complaints register must be produced to ASQA within 7 days of ASQA requesting it production.

the following conditions are imposed on the applicant&rsquo;s registration under s 29 of the NVR Act for its entire registration period and including the period until the outcome of their renewal application is determined (including any review process):

• the applicant&rsquo;s number of enrolled, commenced and studying students from the date of commencement of this condition must not exceed: 3

• 250 students within 6 months of commencement of this condition;

• 350 students within 12 months of commencement of this condition;

• 450 students within 24 months of commencement of this condition;

• 550 students within 36 months of commencement of this condition;

• the applicant must retain and present to ASQA on request any completed pre-enrolment, enrolment, support and progression or training assessment records, for all VET students for all VET courses for a period of two (2) years from the date on which the student ceases to be a student;

• within 6 months of the commencement of this condition, the applicant must engage a suitably qualified independent person to conduct a review and produce a report of its compliance with the Standards for RTOs and National Code and the implementation of its amended training and assessment tools and practices based on a representative sample of the applicant&rsquo;s operations. The review must include observation of practical assessment of a sample of students and the report must be provided to ASQA on request;

• every 6 months the applicant must create, store and produce on request to ASQA, a report of the following information:

• how the RTO ensures its operations are quality assured as required by Standard 2 of the Standards for RTOs, Page 3 of 4 including by detailing, its systems and processes and the implementation of those systems and processes to ensure it complies with each clause of the Standards for RTOs at all times;

• the progress of any compliance monitoring or quality assurance activity it has undertaken in relation to ensuring compliance with each clause of the Standards for RTOs in the preceding 6 months and that it has scheduled in the 6 months; and

• annexing copies of any documents created, used or relied upon to monitor and/or ensure compliance and quality as referred to in that report;

i. the Applicant will commence delivery of training within six months of the date of its registration (either its registration under the ESOS Act or the NVR Act or both, whichever may be the case) and notify the Respondent within fourteen (14) days of that training having commenced;

ii. six months after training has commenced the Applicant will review and evaluate its assessment systems, practices, and strategies to ensure that it has established, implemented, and follows an appropriate assessment system that complies with the relevant training products and is conducted in accordance with the Principles of Assessment and Rules of Evidence;

iii. within eight months after the date of commencing training the Applicant will prepare and complete a report of the review (that is, two months to complete report of the review) which will include showing how it has used the outcomes of the review to continually improve its training and assessment strategies and practices as required by clause 2.2 of the RTO Standards;

iv. thereafter, the Applicant will provide a copy of the report of the review to the Respondent within fourteen (14) days of its completion.

• The RTO must retain copies of:

• enrolment records; and

• all records of completed assessments

• for each student for all TAE training products on the scope of the Applicant&rsquo;s registration for a period of 12 months from the date each student is issued with a TAE qualification by the RTO.

• The RTO retains an Australian Qualifications Framework qualifications issuance register, including the following information:

• student first and surname;

• student contact details (phone number, email address, residential/postal address);

• code and name of course;

• date of enrolment;

• mode of assessment (specific whether &lsquo;Recognition of Prior Learning&rsquo; was used); and

• date of qualification issue.

• The RTO will systematically monitor its operations to ensure ongoing compliance, as required by clause 2.2 of the Standards for RTOs; and will retain records to be produced at any future regulatory activity of the progress of any compliance monitoring or quality assurance activity it has undertaken in the preceding 6 months and that it has scheduled in the upcoming 6 months;

• for the duration of its registration, the RTO will securely retain, and present to ASQA on request, copies of all completed assessment items for each student (per the definition in ASQA's General Direction – Retention requirements for completed student assessment items, which must clearly identify to which student/unit(s) the item relates) and copies of master assessment tools used for each assessment, for a period of 18 months from the date on which the judgment of competence in the relevant unit of competency is made; and

• in addition to complying with the requirements of clause 1.9 of the Standards for RTOs, the applicant will securely retain and present to ASQA on request records that demonstrate that it will, within 12 months of 20 October 2020:

• engage a suitably qualified independent person (who has vocational currency in the training product being validated and the training and assessment qualification or assessor skill set at least to the level being validated) to conduct validation, and the production of a report of that validation, of assessment practices and judgements for each training product, if the product is being currently delivered;

• that validation must be of a representative sample of all students enrolled in the product, must include a minimum of five student files and the report must detail how the sample was determined; and

• the RTO must act on the findings of the report and retain the validation report and records associated with that report and must produce those documents to ASQA on request. For the avoidance of doubt, this includes records of any processes developed or rectifications that occur following the validation process.

The following conditions are imposed on the Applicant&rsquo;s registration under section 29 of the NVR Act:

i. the Applicant must securely retain and present to ASQA on request, all completed student assessment records (including any remedial or gap assessment) for all VET students for all VET courses for 18 months. This condition applies for the duration of the Applicant&rsquo;s registration; and

ii. for 2 years the Applicant must maintain and present to ASQA on request a register of all workplace visits by trainers or assessors. That register must include:

 the name of the workplace;

 the date, time and duration of the visit;

 the name of the trainer or assessor conducting the visit;

 the name and student ID of the student being assessed;

 the name or the student&rsquo;s supervisor and that supervisor&rsquo;s contact details, and

 the units of competency that were assessed.

